        <!-- Q1 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">1. Which of the following best defines the Extracellular Matrix (ECM)?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> An intracellular network of filaments that provides motility to visceral organelles.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> A complex, non-cellular 3D network of proteins and polysaccharides secreted by cells into their surroundings.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> A continuous lipophilic membrane bilayer surrounding individual skeletal muscle fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> A specialized nuclear matrix scaffold responsible for the replication of genomic DNA.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q2 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">2. The non-cellular extracellular matrix provides several vital functions, which include:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Direct ATP synthesis via an alternative anaerobic pathway.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Intracellular degradation of worn-out cytoplasmic organelles.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Storage of glycogen granules and lipid droplets within connective tissue cells.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Structural scaffolding, mechanical strength, biochemical cues for tissue development, and anchoring cells via integrins.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q3 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">3. The composition of the extracellular matrix is divided into three major structural macromolecular classes, which are:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Structural proteins, adhesion proteins, and proteoglycans.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Secondary messengers, fibrous cholesterol, and active enzymes.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Ribosomal units, multi-branched phospholipids, and transport channel gates.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Globular enzymes, nucleoproteins, and free intracellular monosaccharides.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q4 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">4. What is the unique secondary and quaternary conformation characteristic of a mature functional Collagen molecule?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Three right-handed &alpha;-helices twisting together into a left-handed supercoil.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Four alternating parallel pleated sheets running in a counter-clockwise orientation.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Three polypeptide &alpha;-chains (left-handed helices) twisting together into a right-handed supercoil.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> A single long left-handed &alpha;-helix stabilized strictly by intramolecular disulfide bridges.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q5 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">5. The primary amino acid structure of a collagen &alpha;-chain features a strict, unvarying repeating sequence characterized as:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Val-X-Y sequence, where Valine occupies every first position.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Gly-X-Y sequence, where Glycine is present in every third position.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Pro-X-Y sequence, where Proline serves as the exclusive nitrogen donor.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Lys-X-Y sequence, where Lysine forms an unchangeable central core.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q6 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">6. Why is the presence of Glycine at every third position absolutely essential for the structural assembly of the collagen triple helix?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> It is the smallest amino acid, allowing the three chains to pack tightly together into a dense, strong coil.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It carries a strong negative charge that prevents the triple helix from collapsing inward.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It contains a bulky aromatic side chain that pushes the chains apart to maximize total tissue volume.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It forms direct covalent disulfide links with adjacent hyaluronic acid carbohydrate polymers.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q7 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">7. In the standard repeating Gly-X-Y sequence of a collagen &alpha;-chain, which amino acids are most commonly found occupying the X and Y positions?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Alanine is commonly found in the X position, and tryptophan in the Y position.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Lysine is found in the X position, and glycine in the Y position.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Tyrosine is found in the X position, and valine in the Y position.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Proline is commonly found in the X position, and hydroxyproline in the Y position.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

