                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Lysine in the X position, and hydroxylysine in the Y position.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Arginine in the X position, and histidine in the Y position.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q9 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">9. Collagen is a glycosylated protein. Which specific sugar molecules are attached to its structure during post-translational modification?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Glucose and galactose.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Fructose and mannose.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Ribose and deoxyribose.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Sucrose and maltose.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q10 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">10. During the glycosylation process of collagen, glucose and galactose molecules are attached to which specific amino acid residues?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Unmodified Glycine residues occupying the third position.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Hydroxylysine residues.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Proline residues occupying the X position.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Hydroxyproline residues.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q11 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">11. The tremendous tensile strength and resistance to mechanical stress of collagen fibers are achieved through covalent cross-linkages. These links form between:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Hydrophobic side chains of repeating valine and leucine fragments.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Oligosaccharide branched points and neighboring central core residues.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Alpha-carboxyl terminals of alternating glycine segments.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Epsilon-amino groups of lysine and hydroxylysine residues in adjacent chains.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q12 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">12. From a nutritional and dietary perspective, how is collagen protein classified based on its amino acid utility?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Low biological value protein.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> High biological value protein containing all essential amino acids evenly.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Pure non-protein nitrogenous cellular waste product.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Essential fat-emulsifying dietary lipid polymer.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q13 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">13. Macromolecularly, because it contains covalently attached carbohydrate units, collagen is biochemically categorized as a:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Pure unbranched homopolysaccharide devoid of any amino acids.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Proteoglycan monomer containing repeating sulfated disaccharide chains.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Glycoprotein.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Hydrophobic lipid-anchored transmembrane signal receptor.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q14 -->
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">14. Which of the following options represents the primary tissue locations and distribution of Type I Collagen?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Hyaline joint cartilage and internal synovial lubricating fluids.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Bone, skin, tendons, and dentin.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Basal lamina and specialized epithelial clear basement membranes.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Embryonic blood vessels and early fetal skin tissues only.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

